{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, I'm Dr. Lee. I’ll be taking care of you during your stay. Can you tell me what brought you to the hospital today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, Doctor. I had chest pain yesterday while sitting on the couch. It was pressure-like, in the center, and went up to my right shoulder and jaw. I took six nitroglycerin tablets, and it finally eased."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That sounds very concerning. Did you have any shortness of breath, sweating, or nausea with it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A little shortness of breath, especially when I moved. But no nausea or sweating. I’ve had chest pain with exertion before, but this was worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:07:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. We’ve checked your blood work and EKG. Your troponin was elevated, which means your heart muscle was stressed. The EKG showed changes consistent with a mild heart attack—what we call a non-ST elevation MI, or NSTEMI."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A heart attack? But I didn’t have crushing pain like I thought I would. Is it serious?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:11:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, it’s serious, but we caught it early. It means one or more of your heart arteries are narrowed. We’re treating you with aspirin and blood thinners to prevent further damage."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:13:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I remember last time I was here, after my hip surgery, I had something similar. They said it was from low blood pressure."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s right. This time, the pattern is different. Your symptoms and test results suggest active blockage. We’re planning a heart catheterization to look at your arteries more closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m a bit nervous. I had a bad reaction to contrast dye before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve noted that. You’ll get a special pre-treatment with steroids and antihistamines to prevent a reaction. You should be safe."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes me feel better. What about my platelets? I know they’re usually low."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your platelet count is 79, which is within your usual range. We’re monitoring it closely. Because of this, we may avoid placing a stent if possible, to reduce bleeding risk."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I see. So what’s the plan now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll keep you on the heart monitor, check your labs regularly, and prepare for the cath tomorrow. We also did a CT scan of your head because of your history of falls and headaches—no bleeding or stroke was seen."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:29:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good. I’ve been falling a lot lately. I worry about hitting my head again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2150-05-18 20:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve referred you to physical therapy. They’ll assess your balance and help keep you safe. For now, please use the call button before getting up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2150-05-19 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your troponin peaked last night and is now coming down. That’s a good sign—damage has stabilized."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2150-05-19 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a relief. Is the cath still on for today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2150-05-19 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, scheduled for noon. We’ll use contrast, but you’ve already started the pre-medication—prednisone, Zyrtec, and Benadryl—to prevent a reaction."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2150-05-19 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I took those last night and this morning. I feel a bit jittery from the steroid."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2150-05-19 08:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s common. It also raises your white blood cell count—we’ll expect that and not mistake it for infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2150-05-20 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, you’re back from the cath. Everything went well. We found severe blockages in three arteries."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2150-05-20 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Did you open them?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2150-05-20 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We opened the LAD artery with a balloon. The right coronary artery is completely blocked but has good collateral flow, so we left it. The circumflex was too twisted to reach."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2150-05-20 14:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No stent?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2150-05-20 14:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No stent. Because of your low platelets, we avoided it to reduce bleeding risk. You’ll take Plavix for one month instead of the usual longer course."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2150-05-20 14:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. And my other medicines?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2150-05-20 14:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re continuing your usual heart meds. We increased your isosorbide mononitrate from 30 to 60 mg daily to help prevent chest pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2150-05-20 14:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been on that for years. Is it safe at the higher dose?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2150-05-20 14:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, as long as your blood pressure stays stable. We’re watching it closely. You’ve been doing well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2150-05-21 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news. Your latest troponin is down to 0.18, and your heart rhythm is stable. We’re planning to discharge you today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2150-05-21 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. What do I need to do at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2150-05-21 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Continue all your medications, especially the new Plavix for one month. Take nitroglycerin if chest pain returns, and call us if it doesn’t go away after three doses."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2150-05-21 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the increased nitrate pill?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2150-05-21 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, take the 60 mg isosorbide daily. It helps keep your arteries open and reduce angina. Watch for headaches—they’re common but usually improve."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2150-05-21 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I get those already. I also have these falls. Should I see someone?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2150-05-21 09:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Physical therapy cleared you. Keep using your walker, and follow up with your neurologist. Your CT head was normal, so no new injury."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2150-05-21 09:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my heart follow-up?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2150-05-21 09:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "See your cardiologist, Dr. Evans, in one month. They’ll review the cath results and adjust meds if needed. Also, a repeat echo in a year for your aortic size."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2150-05-21 09:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I remember—my aorta was a little enlarged. Is that serious?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2150-05-21 09:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s mild and stable. Just needs monitoring. No surgery now. Focus on blood pressure control and follow-up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2150-05-21 09:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I feel much better knowing what’s going on."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2150-05-21 09:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll have the nurse go over your discharge instructions and medications. Call us if you have chest pain, shortness of breath, or any concerns."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2150-05-21 09:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. And thank you for explaining everything so clearly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2150-05-21 09:28:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Of course. Take care, and we’ll see you in follow-up."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2150-05-18 19:47:00",
    "admission_end": "2150-05-21 16:33:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 68-year-old woman was admitted with acute chest pain radiating to the jaw and shoulder, diagnosed as NSTEMI based on elevated troponin and EKG changes. She underwent cardiac catheterization, which revealed severe three-vessel disease. The LAD artery was dilated with balloon angioplasty, but no stent was placed due to her underlying immune thrombocytopenic purpura and platelet count of 80,000. The RCA was chronically occluded and left untreated, and the circumflex could not be engaged due to tortuosity. She was pre-treated for contrast allergy and tolerated the procedure well. Her isosorbide mononitrate was increased to 60 mg daily, and she was started on a one-month course of clopidogrel. She remained stable, with troponin trending down, and was discharged home in good condition with cardiology follow-up planned.",
    "problems": [
      "Non-ST elevation (NSTEMI) myocardial infarction",
      "Immune thrombocytopenic purpura",
      "Chronic diastolic (congestive) heart failure",
      "Essential (primary) hypertension",
      "Atherosclerotic heart disease of native coronary artery without angina pectoris",
      "Personal history of malignant neoplasm of breast",
      "History of falling"
    ]
  }
}
